`lib/Git/Libgit2/FFI.pm:435-436`:

    _attach git_merge_base_many => [ 'opaque', 'git_repository', 'uint', 'opaque' ] => 'int';
    _attach git_merge_analysis  => [ 'uint*', 'uint*', 'git_repository', 'opaque', 'uint' ] => 'int';

Both counts are `size_t` in C (`git2/merge.h:478-482` `size_t length`; `git2/merge.h:412-417` `size_t their_heads_len`). On x86_64 a 32-bit argument happens to arrive zero-extended, so it works there; on an ABI that does not guarantee the upper half of the slot, libgit2 reads a garbage count and walks off the array. Every other `size_t` parameter in this file is declared `'size_t'`.

Neither function is called anywhere under `t/` (`t/30-merge.t` covers `git_merge_base` only), so these two bindings have never executed.

Done when: both use `'size_t'`, and `t/30-merge.t` exercises `git_merge_base_many` (three tips) and `git_merge_analysis` (a fast-forward and an up-to-date case).

